92186045417] [17592186055898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e9014a35-b28c-5285-9282-5aba8ec6ac64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055899 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055899 #edb/ref 1061 74302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54b5575d-a602-5aa6-b10e-76a946359f7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055900 #edb/ref 1061 74306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "156590f0-8245-5a72-b5e9-95d4b42d678f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1064 "The **greatest common factor** (or ![](images/86/861891fd828ee3558330774b23f5dcee69976d8f302da6f1e6825bf3b4be47e6.svg)) of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is the largest expression that will divide *both* ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we list the factors of the coefficients and then identify any variable factors the expressions have in common.\n\nThe factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) are\n![](images/05/05dbff97b169f70d1dd03beb2c23670b48520c5d98a839d218db50a06518fbf6.svg)\n\nThe factors of ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/59/59919e2659e3bb232e4dbc1857c9f0eca27706bbad72aea6a55bb749706307a2.svg)\n\nThe common factors of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/34/34d666f013d8ce4f2b50c0a9ab9c2b5808feb37163ba38470555867babada4ef.svg) are\n![](images/b0/b08502484874a42582d63ddfc0eda0048576d0cc460aced250ad962721d0e985.svg)\n\nThe greatest of these common factors is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)\n\nNow we look at the variable factors. Since ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) has no variable and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) contains ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) there are no variable factors in common.\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of ![](images/f5/f5a17e5e4e88d2fd8eed0ac09f0e02d77507121fac42d5f696cf407aaab6185c.svg) and ![](images/2f/2fd89ffbc3140e9c2f12576c1dec2522d603c45e8b1d4128c02892f651c6e6bb.svg) is ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1065 1360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b2b71877-6097-5d59-a2b8-7440bd0d5329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055997 #edb/ref 1064 "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055997 #edb/ref 1065 6660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055997 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e82b56d-3e2d-582a-b73f-3c5c98830a58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055997 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055998 #edb/ref 1064 "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055998 #edb/ref 1065 6661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ed2578f7-b1e4-5f83-b963-d66d7b97fa9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055998 #edb/ref 1067 "Names of Polynomials Based on Their Degree" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056006 #edb/ref 1064 "We've already seen that $5^2$ means $5\\cdot 5,$ and that $5^3$ means $5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.$ We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.\n$$\n\nThe **exponent** $({\\color{blue}7})$ says how many times the **base** $(5)$ is multiplied by itself.\n\nRecall that $5^2$ is said as \"five squared\" and that $5^3$ is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say $5^7$  in several different ways:\n\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, $5^{\\color{blue}1}$ means one copy of $5,$ so $5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056006 #edb/ref 1065 8020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056006 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "964b1381-44c4-5a32-832a-ec441cc4a890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056006 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1039 "Splitting a Rational Expression With Two Terms in the Numerator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064257 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "724bdf2f-c9f0-564e-ae33-c64d44c582ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1039 "Splitting a Rational Expression With Three Terms in the Numerator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064258 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0649943a-643f-592f-8815-45c871f7e7dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1045 "Splitting Rational Expressions Into Separate Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064444 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "457bb695-c5a7-5c4b-bc54-87bb84576ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065135 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065135 #edb/ref 1061 18870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "871a576b-9882-5cab-a267-5bfaae09bb5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065136 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065136 #edb/ref 1061 18871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065136 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213c99f3-2e96-5282-8c9f-7798f6d583ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065136 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065137 #edb/ref 1061 18872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065137 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ca88a28-b372-506f-bc28-472bf491340a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065137 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065367 #edb/ref 1064 "Fractions that contain multiple terms in the numerator and a single term in the denominator can be split into separate terms.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {x + 2} {3} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac x 3 + \\dfrac 2 3\n\t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a - 4b + 1} {5} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3a} 5 - \\dfrac {4b} 5 + \\dfrac 1 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nOften, we're able to simplify the fractions in the individual terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x + 9} {2} &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10x} {2} + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t\\dfrac {10} {2} \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\t\n\t\t5 \\cdot x + \\dfrac 9 2 &= \\\n\t\t5x + \\dfrac 9 2 \n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065367 #edb/ref 1065 3375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065367 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a37b6ec-6750-5aea-ae26-ad82d059a0ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065367 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186073033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186073031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073028 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "723fead1-3866-508a-9080-2df0ed86113b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073029 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff3933f1-5330-5a93-bd06-b1