86074896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074895 #edb/ref 1061 81660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aab5d8f4-9d49-5c7f-9619-01557ca3969b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074896 #edb/ref 1061 81663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd74231b-139c-5091-8fd3-66103c7f1329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074897 #edb/ref 1061 81666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b13509a4-8a23-5240-a77b-f79cb3691c99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074898 #edb/ref 1061 8890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "123d2929-5563-5b7e-8588-e7734bb4eb42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074899 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074899 #edb/ref 1061 8891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f954535e-e2c6-5a01-85b8-f90fdccafc1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074900 #edb/ref 1061 8892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074900 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a335606-3bdb-5535-8956-9a3ababd7eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074900 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074901 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074901 #edb/ref 1061 8895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074901 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1961bdcc-cea7-5d78-88c1-4f69e1dab833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074901 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074902 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074902 #edb/ref 1061 8947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d97c5e30-e125-5cba-9973-1e1898eca20e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074968 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2}),$ $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}),$ $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8}).$\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074968 #edb/ref 1065 2053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "05d7bd91-e8b4-5ada-936f-6036f766cf23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074968 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074978 #edb/ref 1064 "The distance between two points in the coordinate plane is the number of units between them. Let's explore two methods for calculating the horizontal distance between two points with the points $(1,1)$ and $(5,1).$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFirst, we plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/19/196c50295e8d8a95084c31226fb9319ede7b70603d894b6939e4615df583c29c.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b531e29616f3e8d7ea0c65761e59cfea224b9c0e756c68b7647c7271dc09493.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](images/6d/6d5c6760e15098dc381e639cd806b6410f2fa6d9049d11d5467427635c9872b6.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{5}},1)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{5}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{5}} - {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074978 #edb/ref 1065 2199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074978 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aeee8d0d-20f6-5700-8c35-c66e0eef8daa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074978 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074979 #edb/ref 1064 "When we want to find the distance between two points in different quadrants, method 1 works exactly the same, but method 2 is slightly different.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis for a horizontal distance, or $x$-axis for a vertical distance, and then *adding*. \n\nSo, what's the distance between $(-3,2)$ and $(1,2)?$\n\n**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e0/e0fec8fca43b421b62efc3d6d892563c80beaab3c2816be47aa7563868555268.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](images/dd/ddc1e6cec323fe245ff2a115a587e773d408dc0115ba5002c02ac1d238620c63.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/0b/0bbb91fc8c0e7ad577261dcdbc8e7aaa16a44e8be2e937e2d6870aae58af4ccf.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{1}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{1}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{-3}},2)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074979 #edb/ref 1065 2204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074979 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1233efd-87e1-5707-9b70-24c24ed47418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074979 #edb/ref 1067 "The Distance Between Two Points in Different Quadrants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a number, its **additive inverse** is the number we need to add to get zero.\nTo find the additive inverse of a number, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it. \n\nFor example, to find the additive inverse of $5,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(5) = -5.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n5+(-5) = 5-5 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1065 15159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "21fd73d9-f9b8-5ef8-9e18-9ba21d6d8d79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the additive inverse of a negative number, we can use the same process.\n\nFor instance, to find the additive inverse of the number $-3,$ we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in front of it.\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{-}}\\,(-3) = 3.\n$$\n\nAgain, we check our work by adding the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n(-3)+3 = -3+3 = 0 \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1065 15161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d30313d5-d29e-5eea-b11c-0619439becb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075030 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Additive Inverse of a Negative Integer" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n![](images/6c/6cda61e0fbd0d9695644a29f0c6fa3478eae84cf253623da7f4b544f35873472.svg)\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10},$ which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\times 2}{10\\times 2} & =\\frac{10}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1065 8357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca7b3d51-7281-5705-bed5-276f34939c25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075038 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186110091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110089 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cfb9f9a8-0793-550e-8000-7cbb65bc3ee6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110090 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110090 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110090 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb0faf0f-5618-54d9-89a7-80c77140df42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110091 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110091 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110091 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f86d63d7-74b3-530e-91d9-8ff31f761445" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
